Below is a compact mathematical formalization and a runnable reference implementation. The six fossils become composable operators inside one semantic reconstruction system.

1. Compression Fossil

The principle is:

Preserve the smallest representation that can reconstruct the important structure.

Let an artifact be x \in \mathcal{X}.

Define:

C : \mathcal{X} \rightarrow \mathcal{Z}

as a compression operator, and:

R : \mathcal{Z} \rightarrow \hat{\mathcal{X}}

as a reconstruction operator.

Successful compression minimizes representation size while preserving reconstruction fidelity:

z^*
=
\arg\min_{z}
\left[
L(z)
+
\lambda D(x,R(z))
\right]

where:

* L(z) is the encoded length of the seed.
* D(x,R(z)) is reconstruction loss.
* \lambda determines how much fidelity matters relative to compactness.

Define semantic leverage:

\Lambda(x)
=
\frac{I(R(C(x)))}{L(C(x))}

where I(\cdot) is the amount of recoverable useful structure.

High leverage means:

\text{small seed}
\quad\rightarrow\quad
\text{large faithful reconstruction}

Pseudocode

FUNCTION COMPRESS(artifact):
    concepts ← extract_repeated_concepts(artifact)
    relations ← extract_high_value_relations(artifact)
    invariants ← find_features_stable_under_transformation(artifact)
    seed ← {
        concepts,
        relations,
        invariants,
        reconstruction_instructions
    }
    WHILE reconstruction_fidelity(seed, artifact) ≥ threshold:
        attempt_to_remove_low_value_component(seed)
    RETURN seed

